<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/foundational/worlds-traditions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Studying religion academically is not believing, debunking or preaching — it is asking how
    traditions actually work: what they claim, how they organise communities, what rituals do,
    and how texts get interpreted. Two ground rules protect that study. First,
    <strong>traditions are plural and changing</strong> — "Hinduism" or "Islam" name vast,
    internally diverse families, not single fixed things. Second, <strong>believers are the
    experts on their own experience</strong>; the scholar's job is comparison and history, not
    verdicts.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="founding moments and turning points across three continents" widget="timeline" config={relTimeline} />

  <h2>What the traditions are asked about</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ultimate reality:</strong> one God, many gods, or no-god-at-all (Buddhism's non-theistic path)? The answers organise everything downstream.</li>
    <li><strong>The human problem:</strong> sin, ignorance, suffering, imbalance — each tradition diagnoses differently, which is why their practices differ.</li>
    <li><strong>Authority:</strong> scripture, priesthood, direct experience, or lineage? Who may interpret is often the real fault line (see the 1054 schism).</li>
    <li><strong>Ritual:</strong> shared meals, prayer, pilgrimage — religion is performed as much as believed, and the performance is what anthropology and sociology study.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why mythology belongs here too</h2>
  <p>
    "Myth" in this field does not mean "false story" — it means the <em>foundational narratives a
    people lives by</em>: creation, flood, hero's journey, founding law-giver. Myths encode how
    societies understood nature, justice and mortality; reading them comparatively is reading
    humanity's oldest self-portraits.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard academic introductions (e.g. Ninian Smart's seven-dimension model); Merneptah Stele scholarship; Eliade, The Sacred and the Profane.</p>
